PersonalFi — `@connector/csv` third-party notices
=================================================

Summary: **no third-party code is vendored in this package.** Every parser here
is an original implementation. This file exists because the work package that
created it was scoped as "port Actual Budget's OFX/QIF parsers (ISC, with
attribution)", and the honest record of why that did not happen is worth more
than an attribution for code that was never copied.

Why nothing was vendored
------------------------

Actual Budget's `server/transactions/import/ofx2json.ts`, `qif2json.ts` and
`xmlcamt2json.ts` are ISC-licensed (`loot-core` is ISC; the repository root is
MIT) and are therefore safe to vendor under `licenses.policy.yaml`, which allows
MIT, BSD, ISC and Apache-2.0.

They were not vendored for one blocking reason: **`ofx2json` depends on
`xml2js`.** Its whole shape is "repair the SGML into well-formed XML, then hand
it to `xml2js`". This work package is constrained to workspace-internal
dependencies, so adding `xml2js` was not available; and a port that drops the
XML library is not a port, it is a rewrite with someone else's copyright header
on it. The reader in `src/ofx.ts` is instead a single-pass tokenizer that treats
SGML and XML as the same grammar (an unclosed leaf is closed when the next tag
opens), which is ~120 lines and needs no XML library at all.

The *idea* — that OFX 1.x SGML can be read by repairing implicit end tags rather
than by writing an SGML parser — is Actual Budget's, and this note is the
acknowledgement. No code, comments, structure or test fixtures were copied.

If that ever changes
--------------------

Rule, for whoever vendors first: **if any ISC-licensed Actual Budget parser code
is vendored into this package, its ISC copyright notice must appear in two
places** — in this file, and as a header comment at the top of every vendored
file, above the SPDX line.

Both, not either. `just verify`'s licence gate reads `pnpm licenses list --prod`,
which sees dependency manifests, and `trivy fs` runs `--scanners
vuln,secret,misconfig`. Neither reads a source header, so nothing in CI can catch
a vendored file that lost its attribution — the per-file notice is the only copy
that travels with the code, and this file is the only copy a reader looks for.

Deliberately not used
---------------------

- **`ofxtools`** (Python) is GPL-3.0-only, despite being widely described as
  permissively licensed (`docs/research/02-ecosystem-vibe-trading.md` §4.5). It
  is on `licenses.policy.yaml`'s `denyPackages` list. It was not consulted.
- **Firefly III's** `import-configurations` are CC-BY-SA-4.0
  (02 §3.1). The CSV presets in `src/presets.ts` were written from the column
  headings of the sample files in `test/fixtures/`, which are also original.
- **Maybe / Sure / Ghostfolio** are AGPL-3.0 (decision D15). Not consulted.

Test fixtures
-------------

Every file under `test/fixtures/` was hand-written for this repository. None
contains real account numbers, real balances, or data from any real institution.

A note on the gate
------------------

Nothing in `just verify` scans source files for third-party licence headers:
`scripts/check-licenses.ts` reads `pnpm licenses list --prod`, and `trivy fs`
runs with `--scanners vuln,secret,misconfig` — `license` is not enabled. So a
vendored ISC file would pass the gate today with no attribution recorded
anywhere. That gap is real whether or not this package uses it; it is written up
in `docs/evidence/phase-1/wp1.2-connections.md` under "Deferred".
